  "final_text": "Both objects exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-16f9e042 Web logs overview\"** (id `9f63d4a7-c4a6-4943-a636-7d5d828c1c26`), six panels on the Kibana Sample Data Logs data view:\n- Total requests (count)\n- Bytes served (sum of `bytes`)\n- Unique visitors (unique count of `clientip`)\n- 5xx responses (count where `response.keyword` is 500\u2013599)\n- Requests over time (date histogram of count on `@timestamp`)\n- Responses by status code (count by `response.keyword`)\n\n**Rule: \"EFF-16f9e042 5xx spike\"** (id `8a26b06a-dada-4639-9868-bedd26f25115`), Elasticsearch query rule type:\n- Runs every 1 minute.\n- Counts documents in `kibana_sample_data_logs` where `response.keyword` is 500\u2013599, over a 5-minute window.\n- Fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- Enabled. Its first run finished with status `ok`.\n- Has no actions attached, so it will record alerts but won't notify anyone.\n\n**Field exploration:** I used the data view's field mapping for the choices above. The Kibana internal search, console proxy, and ES|QL endpoints are disabled here, and Elasticsearch isn't reachable on its usual ports, so I couldn't run ad-hoc queries to check the actual values. Whether the 5xx panel and rule match real data is unverified. Open the dashboard in Kibana to confirm the panels render values.\n\n**Choices I made:**\n- The 5xx match uses a lexical range on `response.keyword` (500\u2013599) rather than a numeric field. It works for three-digit codes.\n- I added a status-code breakdown and a unique-visitors panel beyond the minimum three panels, since they're the key traffic metrics for this data.",
